  "final_report": "[stop_reason: hop_cap_reached | hops used: 4/4]\n\nAnalyst summary:\n**What we know about the August\u202f2017 Dow disruption**\n\n| Tier | Company (sector) | Why it appears at risk (structural link) | Evidence found |\n|------|-------------------|-------------------------------------------|----------------|\n| **Tier\u202f1** | Avient (specialty polymer & colorants) | Direct downstream customer of Dow\u2019s specialty\u2011polymer business. | *No filing or corpus mention that references a Dow disruption.* |\n|  | Formosa Plastics (PVC resin, epichlorohydrin) | Purchases basic chemicals from Dow. | *No filing or corpus mention that references a Dow disruption.* |\n|  | Hyundai Motor (passenger\u2011vehicle manufacturer) | Uses engineered plastics and coatings supplied by Dow. | **get_supplier_info** returned \u201cno filings on record for \u2018Hyundai Motor\u2019.\u201d \u2013 no direct evidence of impact. |\n|  | Samsung Electronics (automotive electronics, displays) | Relies on Dow\u2019s advanced materials for semiconductor & display substrates. | *No filing or corpus mention that references a Dow disruption.* |\n|  | Saudi Arabian Oil (crude & petroleum products) | Purchases petrochemical feedstocks from Dow\u2019s refining\u2011related units. | *No filing or corpus mention that references a Dow disruption.* |\n| **Tier\u202f2** (selected examples) | Apple (technology hardware) | Uses Dow\u2011derived polymers and specialty chemicals in device casings, adhesives, etc. | **get_supplier_info** returned Apple\u2019s 2016 Form\u202f10\u2011K \u2013 filed more than a year before the August\u202f2017 event; it contains no reference to any Dow supply issue. |\n|  | Toyota Tsusho (automotive metals & materials trading) | Trades Dow\u2011produced engineering plastics and composites. | *No filing or corpus mention that references a Dow disruption.* |\n|  | LG Chem (petrochemicals, battery cathode material) | Sources basic chemicals from Dow\u2019s petrochemical platform. | *No filing or corpus mention that references a Dow disruption.* |\n|  | ZF\u202fTRW Automotive (automotive safety systems) | Uses Dow\u2011made polymers and high\u2011performance resins in airbags & seat\u2011belt components. | *No filing or corpus mention that references a Dow disruption.* |\n|  | Mercedes\u2011Benz (premium passenger cars) | Relies on Dow\u2019s advanced plastics for interior/exterior parts. | *No filing or corpus mention that references a Dow disruption.* |\n\n**How the evidence was gathered**\n\n1. **Structural mapping** \u2013 The first tool call (`traverse_supply_graph`, downstream, max\u202ftier\u202f=\u202f2) identified 79 enriched companies (Tier\u202f1 and Tier\u202f2) that are directly or indirectly supplied by Dow. This list is the *complete set of companies that could be structurally at risk* given a supply\u2011side disruption at Dow.\n\n2. **Search for explicit mentions** \u2013 A second tool call (`search_corpus` for \u201cDow disruption\u201d around 2017\u201108\u201125) returned ten results, none of which specifically linked any downstream company to the August\u202f2017 Dow event. The hits were generic risk\u2011management language from unrelated firms (BP, Avient, etc.) and did not confirm that the listed downstream companies experienced a material impact.\n\n3. **Company\u2011specific filings** \u2013 Two `get_supplier_info` calls were made for the most prominent Tier\u202f1 candidates:  \n   * **Hyundai Motor** \u2013 No SEC filing was found for the company at all, so no direct statement about any Dow\u2011related supply issue could be retrieved.  \n   * **Apple** \u2013 The only filing returned was Apple\u2019s 2016 Form\u202f10\u2011K (filed 303\u202fdays before the event). It contains no reference to a Dow disruption; therefore it cannot serve as evidence of impact.\n\n**What we could not confirm**\n\n* No downstream company in Tier\u202f1 or Tier\u202f2 has publicly disclosed, within its SEC filings, that it suffered a material supply interruption from Dow in August\u202f2017.  \n* The corpus search did not surface any news\u2011release, risk\u2011factor, or management discussion that ties the listed companies to a Dow disruption on the target date.  \n* For many of the Tier\u202f1/Tier\u202f2 firms (e.g., Formosa Plastics, Samsung Electronics, Saudi Arabian Oil, Toyota Tsusho, LG Chem, ZF\u202fTRW Automotive, Mercedes\u2011Benz) we have **no** filing or corpus excerpt that mentions Dow at all in the relevant timeframe.  \n* The investigation ran out of tool calls before we could probe each company individually; consequently, a large portion of the structurally\u2011at\u2011risk list remains *unverified*.\n\n**Bottom line**\n\n- Structurally, dozens of downstream manufacturers and material traders (Avient, Formosa Plastics, Hyundai Motor, Samsung Electronics, Saudi Arabian Oil, Apple, Toyota\u202fTsusho, LG\u202fChem, ZF\u202fTRW Automotive, Mercedes\u2011Benz, etc.) could be affected by a supply disruption at Dow.  \n- **However, with the evidence available from SEC filings and the corpus search, we found no concrete public confirmation that any of these companies actually experienced a material impact from the August\u202f2017 Dow event.**  \n\nBecause of the four\u2011call limit, the investigation is necessarily incomplete: many downstream firms could not be individually checked, and none of the sources consulted provided explicit proof of an operational or financial effect stemming from the Dow disruption. Further research (e.g., targeted news\u2011article searches, later SEC filings, or direct company disclosures) would be required to determine which, if any, of these structurally\u2011linked firms truly felt the impact.\n\nCOVERAGE GAPS (76) -- reported explicitly, not omitted:\n  - formosa plastics: no evidence gathered\n  - nan ya plastics: no evidence gathered\n  - hyundai motor: no evidence gathered\n  - samsung electronics: no evidence gathered\n  - gr